-- What Is Architectural Visualization? A Complete Guide  (en)
do $do$
declare pid uuid;
begin
  select post_id into pid from public.blog_post_translations where locale = 'en' and slug = $v$what-is-architectural-visualization$v$;
  if pid is null then
    insert into public.blog_posts (status) values ('draft') returning id into pid;
  end if;
  update public.blog_posts set
    status = 'published', publish_at = coalesce(publish_at, now()),
    category_id = (select id from public.blog_categories where slug = $v$3d-visualization$v$),
    author_id = (select id from public.blog_authors where slug = 'vari-studio'),
    cover = $v$8b4d4f_52780c64f74a4a3dac703141b59358c2~mv2.png$v$,
    cover_alt = $v${"en": "Daylight visualization of the timber-clad HNE Laboratory in Germany, framed by trees and people for scale"}$v$::jsonb,
    faq = $v$[{"q": {"en": "What does an architectural visualizer do?"}, "a": {"en": "They turn drawings and design intent into images, films or interactive models. That means modelling the project, assigning materials, lighting the scene, choosing camera views, rendering and finishing the images."}}, {"q": {"en": "Is architectural visualization the same as architectural rendering?"}, "a": {"en": "In everyday use, almost. Rendering strictly means the computer calculation that produces an image; visualization is the whole discipline, including animation, virtual tours and interactive work."}}, {"q": {"en": "What software is used for architectural visualization?"}, "a": {"en": "Common choices include V-Ray, Corona, Enscape, D5 Render, Lumion, Twinmotion, Unreal Engine and Blender. Each suits a different mix of quality, speed and workflow."}}, {"q": {"en": "How long does an architectural rendering take?"}, "a": {"en": "It depends on the modelling required and the number of views. A single still from clean files can take days; a full series, an animation or an interactive model takes longer. Studios usually work to a schedule with approval stages."}}, {"q": {"en": "Do I need a 3D model to get a visualization?"}, "a": {"en": "No. Studios regularly work from 2D drawings, such as plans, elevations and sections, together with material lists and reference images. An existing 3D model speeds things up."}}]$v$::jsonb
  where id = pid;
  insert into public.blog_post_translations (post_id, locale, slug, title, excerpt, html, takeaways, seo_title, seo_description, focus_keyword, ready, word_count)
  values
  (pid, $v$en$v$, $v$what-is-architectural-visualization$v$, $v$What Is Architectural Visualization? A Complete Guide$v$, $v$What architectural visualization is, how a render is made, the formats it comes in, who uses it and why, explained by a studio that makes them every day.$v$, $v$<p>Architectural visualization is the craft of showing a building, an interior or a landscape before it exists, in images people can understand without reading drawings. Most of it today is computer-generated: a 3D model of the design is given materials, light and a camera, and a rendering engine calculates what that scene would look like. The result might be a single photorealistic image, a film, a 360° tour or an interactive model.</p>
<p>The discipline is often shortened to <em>archviz</em>, and the people who practise it are called 3D visualizers, visualization artists or CGI artists. It sits between architecture, photography and film, and borrows from all three.</p>
<h2>What it is used for</h2>
<p>Visualization answers a different question at each stage of a project.</p>
<p><strong>Design.</strong> Architects and interior designers use images to test their own ideas: does the façade rhythm work, is the stair too heavy, does the room feel as calm as the plan suggests?</p>
<p><strong>Decisions.</strong> Clients approve what they can see. A rendered option of two kitchen stones, or two cladding colours, turns a long discussion into a short one.</p>
<p><strong>Competitions and approvals.</strong> Juries, planning committees and neighbours judge proposals through images, which is why accuracy and context matter so much at this stage.</p>
<p><strong>Selling and funding.</strong> Developers market apartments that are still a construction site, and investors fund schemes they can only picture. Here the images become, in effect, the product, and a promise. We look at the responsibilities that come with that in <a href="/en/blog/renders-vs-reality">Renders vs Reality</a>.</p>
<h2>The formats</h2>
<p><strong>Still images</strong> are the workhorse: one carefully composed view, like an architectural photograph. <strong>Animations</strong> move a camera through and around a project to show arrival, sequence and scale. <strong>360° panoramas</strong> let a viewer look around from one point; <strong>virtual tours</strong> link several of them. <strong>Real-time and VR</strong> experiences let people walk freely through a model, often in a sales gallery or on a website.</p>
<p>Each format has a job. Our comparison of <a href="/en/blog/3d-rendering-vs-animation-vs-virtual-tour">rendering, animation and virtual tours</a> goes through when each one earns its cost.</p>
<h2>How an image is made</h2>
<p>Behind every photorealistic image is the same sequence, whatever the software.</p>
<p><strong>The model.</strong> The geometry is built from the project's drawings or imported from CAD and BIM files. Everything that should appear has to exist in 3D: walls and window frames, but also furniture, planting, people and the street.</p>
<p><strong>Materials.</strong> Surfaces are described physically: how rough the stone is, how much light the timber reflects, how glass bends what is behind it. Physically based materials are why modern renders look convincing under any light.</p>
<p><strong>Light.</strong> A sun position, a sky, interior fixtures. Light is the single biggest decision in an image: it sets the time of day, the mood and what the eye notices first.</p>
<p><strong>Camera.</strong> Position, height and lens decide how much of the space is shown and how large it feels. A good visualizer frames a view the way an architectural photographer would.</p>
<p><strong>Rendering.</strong> The engine calculates how light travels through the scene. <em>Offline</em> renderers trade time for accuracy and produce final images. <em>Real-time</em> engines produce results instantly and are used for walkthroughs and interactive work. The tools are compared in our guide to <a href="/en/blog/best-rendering-software-for-architecture">the best rendering software for architecture</a>.</p>
<p><strong>Post-production.</strong> Colour, contrast and small corrections, the equivalent of developing a photograph.</p>
<p>In a professional studio these steps are approved in stages: first the cameras on simple grey models, then materials and light, then the final images. That way a change of view doesn't arrive after a week of detailed work.</p>
<h2>Who makes it</h2>
<p>Architecture firms sometimes keep visualizers in-house. Many work with freelancers or specialised studios, locally or abroad. Our guide to <a href="/en/blog/outsourcing-architectural-rendering">outsourcing architectural rendering</a> covers how that relationship works in practice, and <a href="/en/blog/how-to-choose-a-3d-rendering-studio">how to choose a 3D rendering studio</a> covers the selection. For the studios the industry keeps recognising, see <a href="/en/blog/best-architectural-visualization-studios">the best architectural visualization studios</a>.</p>
<h2>What makes visualization good</h2>
<p>Beautiful light and materials are the visible part. The less visible part matters as much: images that match the drawings, a series in which every view shows the same building, and honesty about what will really be built. A render that wins the sale and loses the buyer's trust at handover has not done its job.</p>
<h2>Where AI fits</h2>
<p>Generative AI has changed the earliest stage of design. It can produce convincing images from a sentence or a sketch in seconds, which is excellent for exploring ideas. It is much weaker at holding one design steady across many views or matching real dimensions. We explain where each approach belongs in <a href="/en/blog/ai-architectural-rendering-vs-studio">AI rendering vs a professional studio</a>.</p>
<h2>What it costs</h2>
<p>Prices depend mostly on the modelling effort, the number of views and the level of realism. Still images are usually priced per view, animations per second or minute. Our <a href="/en/blog/architectural-rendering-cost">architectural rendering cost guide</a> gives published 2026 ranges and what moves them.</p>
<h2>A few terms you will hear</h2>
<p><strong>Render</strong>: a computer-calculated image. <strong>CGI</strong>: computer-generated imagery. <strong>Clay render</strong>: a draft image without materials, used to approve cameras. <strong>HDRI</strong>: a panoramic photograph of a sky used to light a scene. <strong>PBR</strong>: physically based rendering, the material approach behind modern realism. <strong>Walkthrough / flythrough</strong>: an animation through or over a project. <strong>Real-time</strong>: rendering fast enough to move through a scene interactively.</p>
<p>At VARI we work across architecture, interior design and visualization, from 2D drafting and BIM to renders, animation and virtual tours. You can see the results in <a href="/en/work">our projects</a> and the full range under <a href="/en/services">services</a>.</p>
<h2>References</h2>
<ul><li>ArchDaily, "Get to Know These Distinguished Architectural Visualization Studios and Their Artwork" (2020): archdaily.com/941488</li><li>CGArchitect 3D Awards (annual industry awards for architectural visualization): cgarchitect.com</li></ul>$v$, $v$["Architectural visualization shows a design before it exists, mostly as computer-generated images, films or interactive models.", "It serves design, decisions, competitions and approvals, and sales.", "Every image follows the same steps: model, materials, light, camera, rendering, post-production.", "Good visualization is accurate and consistent across a series, not just beautiful."]$v$::jsonb, $v$What Is Architectural Visualization? A Complete Guide$v$, $v$What architectural visualization is, how a render is made, the formats it comes in, who uses it and why, explained by a studio that makes them every day.$v$, $v$what is architectural visualization$v$, true, 942)
