-- Outsourcing Architectural Rendering: A Practical Guide for Architecture Firms  (en)
do $do$
declare pid uuid;
begin
  select post_id into pid from public.blog_post_translations where locale = 'en' and slug = $v$outsourcing-architectural-rendering$v$;
  if pid is null then
    insert into public.blog_posts (status) values ('draft') returning id into pid;
  end if;
  update public.blog_posts set
    status = 'published', publish_at = coalesce(publish_at, now()),
    category_id = (select id from public.blog_categories where slug = $v$guides$v$),
    author_id = (select id from public.blog_authors where slug = 'vari-studio'),
    cover = $v$8b4d4f_d8c978fb79044dd5b22cf9f8edca55b7~mv2.png$v$,
    cover_alt = $v${"en": "Roof & Garden, an exterior residential visualization by VARI Studio"}$v$::jsonb,
    faq = $v$[{"q": {"en": "Is it cheaper to outsource architectural rendering?"}, "a": {"en": "For uneven or occasional demand, usually yes: you pay per image instead of a salary, software, hardware and idle time. For daily rendering inside the design process, an in-house visualizer can be more economical."}}, {"q": {"en": "What files does a rendering studio need?"}, "a": {"en": "Plans, elevations and sections (DWG, PDF or a 3D model), a finishes list, site information and a few reference images, plus a note on how the images will be used."}}, {"q": {"en": "How many revisions are normal?"}, "a": {"en": "Many studios include two or three rounds. Agree in writing what counts as a revision and what counts as a design change."}}, {"q": {"en": "Is it safe to share drawings with a studio abroad?"}, "a": {"en": "Yes, with a confidentiality agreement, a secure file-transfer method and clear usage terms agreed before the project starts."}}, {"q": {"en": "How long does an outsourced rendering take?"}, "a": {"en": "It depends on modelling effort and the number of views. A single still from clean files often takes days; ask for a schedule with dates for draft cameras and final images."}}]$v$::jsonb
  where id = pid;
  insert into public.blog_post_translations (post_id, locale, slug, title, excerpt, html, takeaways, seo_title, seo_description, focus_keyword, ready, word_count)
  values
  (pid, $v$en$v$, $v$outsourcing-architectural-rendering$v$, $v$Outsourcing Architectural Rendering: A Practical Guide for Architecture Firms$v$, $v$When to outsource visualization, what it really costs compared with in-house, how to brief a remote studio, and how time zones, revisions and rights work.$v$, $v$<p>Most architecture firms reach the same point sooner or later: the design is ready, the presentation is on Thursday, and nobody in the office has three uninterrupted days to model, light and render it. Some firms answer by hiring a visualizer. Many answer by outsourcing. Both can work. The difference lies in how well the arrangement is set up.</p>
<h2>Three ways to get renders</h2>
<p><strong>In-house.</strong> A visualizer on the team knows your projects, your standards and your people. The cost is more than a salary: software licences, a powerful workstation, training, and the weeks when there is little to render and the weeks when there is far too much.</p>
<p><strong>Freelancers.</strong> Flexible and often cost-effective for a single image or a small project. The risk is capacity: one person, one set of holidays, one hard drive.</p>
<p><strong>Specialised studios.</strong> A team with established processes, several artists who can share a deadline, and experience across many building types. You pay for that structure, and you gain its reliability.</p>
<p>Many firms combine them: a simple real-time tool in-house for design reviews, and a studio for competition entries, planning images and marketing.</p>
<h2>When outsourcing makes sense</h2>
<p>Outsourcing tends to pay off when visualization demand is uneven, when the stakes of an image are high (a competition, a planning committee, a sales launch), or when a project needs formats your team doesn't produce, such as animation or virtual tours.</p>
<p>It makes less sense when images are needed daily as part of the design process itself. There, a visualizer in the room, or a real-time renderer in your modelling software, is hard to beat.</p>
<h2>The handover: what to send</h2>
<p>The quality of what comes back is decided largely by what goes out. A good handover includes:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Drawings</strong>: plans, elevations and sections as DWG, PDF or an existing model (Revit, ArchiCAD, SketchUp, Rhino).</li><li><strong>Materials</strong>: a finishes list, ideally with product names or samples.</li><li><strong>Context</strong>: site plan, photos of the site, neighbouring heights, orientation.</li><li><strong>References</strong>: two or three images that show the mood you want, and a note on what you don't want.</li><li><strong>Purpose</strong>: who will see the images and where (jury, planning, brochure, billboard), because that decides resolution, format and how neutral or atmospheric the image should be.</li><li><strong>Decisions</strong>: which parts of the design are fixed and which are still open.</li></ul>
<h2>Approval stages that prevent rework</h2>
<p>Revisions are where outsourced projects go over budget, almost always because a big decision is made late. A staged process avoids it:</p>
<p>1. <strong>File check and questions</strong>, before anything is modelled. 2. <strong>Draft cameras</strong> on simple grey models: agree the views now, while changing them is cheap. 3. <strong>Materials and light</strong>: the stage that makes the image believable. 4. <strong>Final images</strong> and post-production.</p>
<p>Agree in writing how many revision rounds are included, what counts as a revision (lighter, warmer, a different chair) and what counts as a design change (a new façade), which is quoted separately.</p>
<h2>Time zones: overlap beats "overnight"</h2>
<p>Some offers promise that work happens while you sleep. In practice, visualization depends on quick questions and quick answers, so <strong>shared working hours</strong> are worth more than overnight delivery. A studio whose day overlaps yours can resolve a question in ten minutes rather than losing a day.</p>
<p>For example, Cairo is one hour ahead of Central Europe for most of the year and one to two hours behind the Gulf, so a Cairo studio shares almost the whole working day with both. Language matters just as much. Check that you can review drafts in the language your team works in.</p>
<h2>Confidentiality, ownership and usage rights</h2>
<p>Before the first file is sent, agree:</p>
<ul><li><strong>Confidentiality</strong>, especially for competition entries and projects not yet public.</li><li><strong>Usage rights</strong>: where you may use the images (web, print, portals, advertising) and for how long.</li><li><strong>Portfolio use</strong>: whether, and from when, the studio may publish the work.</li><li><strong>Source files</strong>: whether you receive the 3D model, layered files or only final images.</li></ul>
<h2>Pricing models</h2>
<p><strong>Per image</strong> is the most common and the easiest to compare. Additional views of the same model usually cost less than the first, because the modelling is already done. <strong>Per project</strong> suits a defined package, for example six stills and a short animation. <strong>Retainers</strong> suit firms with steady demand who want reserved capacity. What drives the price, and published 2026 ranges, are in our <a href="/en/blog/architectural-rendering-cost">architectural rendering cost guide</a>.</p>
<h2>Making the first project a good one</h2>
<p>Start with a project that has a clear brief and a reasonable deadline rather than the most urgent one. Judge the studio on communication as much as on the final image: did they ask the right questions, keep to the stages, and handle feedback well? Our <a href="/en/blog/how-to-choose-a-3d-rendering-studio">10-point checklist for choosing a rendering studio</a> covers the selection itself.</p>
<h2>How we work with firms</h2>
<p>At VARI we work from our studio in Cairo with architects, developers and designers in Europe and North America. Our portfolio includes projects in Germany, the Netherlands, Norway, Canada and the USA. Communication is possible in English, German and Spanish. We build images from your drawings, in stages: file check, draft cameras, materials and light, final images. We can mark every camera position on the plan, and alongside any dusk image we deliver a daylight version of the same view. If that sounds like the right fit, <a href="/en/contact">tell us about your project</a>.</p>$v$, $v$["Outsourcing pays off for uneven demand, high-stakes images and formats your team does not produce.", "A complete handover (drawings, materials, context, references, purpose) decides the result.", "Staged approvals, starting with draft cameras, prevent costly rework.", "Shared working hours and a common language matter more than overnight promises."]$v$::jsonb, $v$Outsourcing Architectural Rendering: A Guide for Firms$v$, $v$When to outsource visualization, what it really costs compared with in-house, how to brief a remote studio, and how time zones, revisions and rights work.$v$, $v$outsource architectural rendering$v$, true, 898)
